@page "/prose"

<CatHead Title="Prose">
    Long-form text the app did not author &mdash; a release note, a knowledge-base article, an
    e-mail body &mdash; and a value the reader is not allowed to see. Rendered Markdown has its own
    class, <a href="/markdown"><code>.markdown-body</code></a>, and looks identical.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Prose.Prose" Title="Prose" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.prose</code> normalises headings, paragraphs, lists, quotes, code and links onto the
        tokens, for HTML that did not come from Markdown. <strong>The 72-character measure sits on
        the element carrying the class</strong>, so where the prose <em>is</em> a panel &mdash;
        <code>class="card-body prose"</code> &mdash; set <code>--prose-measure: none</code> on the
        box and put the class on an inner wrapper.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Prose.Foreign" Title="HTML from somewhere else" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.prose--foreign</code> adds <strong>containment</strong> on top of
        <code>.prose</code>'s typography: what a 3000px-wide table, an unbroken 400-character URL or
        a 4000px image does to the card it landed in. Add it to anything sanitized rather than
        authored — a ticketing system's journal entry, a forwarded mail body, a CMS field — and note
        that it contains but does not sanitize: the same server-side pass has to add
        <code>tabindex="0"</code> to a table that scrolls, or a keyboard cannot reach the off-screen
        columns, and <code>role="table"</code> to put back the role
        <code>display: block</code> drops.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Prose.Changes" Title="What changed" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>&lt;ins&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;del&gt;</code> mark the words added and removed
        between two versions of a text &mdash; a policy, a manual page, a contract &mdash; in prose
        or in any running text, with no class. The tint is said again by an underline and a
        strike-through, so the change survives forced colours, print and a reader who cannot tell
        green from red; a change that wraps keeps both ends on every line, and the line height does
        not move. What a screen reader says is yours: a <code>.visually-hidden</code> "added:" or
        "removed:" inside the element, in your language.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Prose.Redacted" Title="Redacted values" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A value deliberately withheld — a secret, a masked identifier, a field the reader's role
        cannot see. <strong>It must not merely look hidden:</strong> this class marks a placeholder
        the server sent <em>in place of</em> the value, so if the real text is in the HTML it is not
        redacted, whatever it looks like.
    </p>
</CatExample>
